  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot temperatures, high humidity, and severe \nthunderstorm chances again today into Saturday.  \n\nAdditional showers and thunderstorms, possibly severe, are \nanticipated again this afternoon/evening. Timing is a bit uncertain \ngiven the subtle waves aloft, but increasing low-level convergence \npaired with another upper impulse may very well result in another \nround or two of storms with a damaging wind risk. The latest \nguidance has nudged the threat a bit further north due to slower \nprogression of the cold front, encompassing areas as far north as I-\n70 or the Mason-Dixon line . While storms can develop over the \nregion and congeal before moving southeast, areas in Ohio, \nPennsylvania, and West Virginia will need to be monitored to see if \nanything grows upscale in those areas in linear segments and\ndives southeast. \n\nHighs today will range from the upper 80s to lower 90s with dew \npoints in the lower to middle 70s resulting in heat indices in the \nupper 90s to lower 100s. This will be just shy of heat headline \ncriteria (except in Charlottesville and surrounding Albemarle/Nelson \nCounties). The potential for early day clouds to offset heating \nprecludes enough confidence to issue any further heat headlines at \nthis time, though it will be unusually hot and humid for early \nSeptember regardless.\n\nNotably cooler Sat with some showers or thunderstorms associated \nwith a disturbance aloft. There remains notable uncertainty wrt \ncoverage. Lower odds for SVR wx Sat with SPC only having the FA in \nGeneral Thunder. Some models much more bullish than others, with the \ngreatest likelihood of precip across the SW half of the FA. Highs \nSat in the 80s for most. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions expected for the second half of \nLabor Day weekend through early next week.\n\nSfc high pressure over the GL will track over the Mid-Atlantic Sun \ninto Mon, with accompanying mid-level dry air that will bring down \nhumidity and t-storm chances. Daily highs drop to the low 80s, along \nwith drier dewpoints in the mid to upper 50s. These conditions \nlikely persist through Tue as high pressure drifts south.\n\nAs high pressure dominates, expect temps to slowly creep back up to \nthe mid-to-upper 80s beginning Tue and Wed. Entering into the \nmidweek, broad upper-level ridging begins to build over the southern \nPlains, with a deep trough forming to the north over Nova Scotia. \nThis will create a frontal boundary tracking across the East Coast \nsometime during this period, potentially beginning Wed and into Thu. \nThis could create chances for a few showers or storms to form along \nthe front as it tracks through, along with increasing humidity.",
